# Skill priority and coexistence

## Stack context

This project is a **Vite + React 18 + TypeScript** SPA with **react-router-dom**, **Tailwind**, and **shadcn/ui** — not Next.js and not React Native. Apply Vercel React skills for generic React patterns; ignore Next.js App Router, RSC, and React Native guidance unless the stack changes.

Preserve the existing **Pick Six** visual identity unless the user asks for a redesign.

## Writing (highest priority)

**stop-slop** has priority over all other skills for **wording, tone, and prose** (replies, UI copy, docs, commit/PR text).

When explaining code or debugging, also follow `.cursor/rules/stop-slop-technical.mdc` (technical hedges, passive voice for architecture, scannable lists, code citations).

User Rules and git/PR workflows still win over stylistic preferences.

## Visual / UI design (coexist, resolve conflicts)

Use **ui-ux-pro-max** and **frontend-design** together when possible:

- **ui-ux-pro-max**: accessibility, layout, spacing, interaction states, typography tokens, UX audits, shadcn/Tailwind patterns, product-appropriate style selection.
- **frontend-design**: distinctive, production-grade visual direction; avoid generic AI aesthetics when building or beautifying UI.

**On conflict, ui-ux-pro-max wins** over frontend-design (accessibility, contrast, touch targets, form UX, responsive rules, and established project patterns take precedence).

frontend-design applies for creative polish and visual distinctiveness only where it does not violate ui-ux-pro-max or the current brand.

## React code quality

- **vercel-react-best-practices**: performance, data fetching patterns, bundle/list optimization (Vite-appropriate subset).
- **vercel-composition-patterns**: component APIs, compound components, composition over boolean prop sprawl.

These apply to implementation and refactors; they do not override ui-ux-pro-max for visual/UX decisions or stop-slop for prose.
